---
.title = "Configuration",
.description = "Turning overwatch off, or giving a project its own commands, with .overwatch.json.",
.date = .date("2026-10-21T00:00:06"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
---


`.overwatch.json` at the project root, every field optional. Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Pi, and Neovim all read it; `summary` and `failed` are JavaScript-style regexes, which the Neovim plugin translates.

```json
{
  "enabled": true,
  "argv": ["zig", "build", "test", "--watch"],
  "baseline": ["zig", "build", "test"],
  "summary": "^Build Summary:.*$",
  "failed": "failed"
}
```

`"enabled": false` turns overwatch off for that project, zanity included; `"baseline": []` skips the full run first. The other fields apply to one test watcher alone: in a polyglot project or a monorepo, the first language in the table above that the project has, at its root or in a sub-project. The override is read only at the project's root.

`watchers` configures any entry of `watchers.json` by its name, test watchers and checkers alike, in a polyglot project or a monorepo too, where a sub-project's watcher takes its entry's settings. Each takes `enabled`, `argv`, `baseline`, `summary`, `failed` and `env`, variables set for its commands over overwatch's own and merged over the entry's. An entry turned off gives way to the next of its language, so turning off `vitest` runs `jest` or `bun` when the project has one. The top-level fields above apply after these. Codex, OpenCode, and Pi refuse a setting of the wrong type, or a field it does not take, and name it.

```json
{
  "watchers": {
    "git": { "env": { "OVERWATCH_GIT_INTERVAL": "300" } },
    "zanity": { "enabled": false },
    "python": { "baseline": ["uv", "run", "pytest", "-m", "not slow"] }
  }
}
```

A project with no marker file is watched when its override names `summary`, `failed`, and `argv` or `baseline`; `name` labels it in the status line. With no `argv`, overwatch needs no watch tool: it reruns `baseline` itself after the agent's edits (Edit, Write, MultiEdit, NotebookEdit or Bash calls) go quiet. An edit made while the suite runs stops that run and starts another, so the agent never hears a result its later edits have already overtaken. Edits made outside the session are picked up at the agent's next edit. This repository watches its own tests that way:

```json
{
  "name": "plugin",
  "baseline": ["claude", "plugin", "test", "."],
  "summary": "^ \\d+ fail$",
  "failed": "^[1-9]\\d* fail$"
}
```

# [Legacy-code triage]($heading.id('legacy-code-triage'))

A legacy project can start with thousands of errors. Overwatch records the first result of each check as inherited backlog and sends one short notice with the inventory path. It saves all named issues and the full original output outside the conversation. Fixing that backlog is deliberate work; by default, it does not prevent ordinary tools or Stop events. New failures keep completion guarded, even if other errors were already present. Updating results never replaces the initial baseline with new failures. A pull or checkout that moves HEAD under the session brings in failures the agent did not make, so each check reruns and its next result becomes the baseline; in Claude Code, unless the agent edits first. A move made only by commits, which bring in no files, keeps the baseline, so an edit committed in the same step as the check breaks is still a new failure. Overwatch reads the HEAD reflog to tell the two apart; when it cannot, the move takes a fresh baseline. So does a step whose shell command runs a git operation that can bring in files (`merge`, `pull`, `cherry-pick`, `rebase`, `checkout`, `submodule` and the like) or `gh pr checkout`, `gh pr merge` or `gh repo sync`, even when it ends in a commit, as `git merge --squash` does. Overwatch reads the command as a shell does: operators, parentheses, `$(…)`, backticks and `sh -c` scripts are followed, and quoted text, such as a commit message that names a merge, is not a command. A command that hides such an operation in a script file, a git alias, or a command substitution inside double quotes is read as a commit. Also in Claude Code, the first full run can still be going when the agent starts editing; edits made during it are rechecked once it ends, and that first result is no baseline, since it may hold those edits, so every failure it reports counts as new.

Inspect a small batch from the inventory path in the notice:

```sh
python3 hooks/triage.py /path/from/notice.baseline.json --limit 20
python3 hooks/triage.py /path/from/notice.baseline.json --check go --match TestAdd --limit 10
python3 hooks/triage.py /path/from/notice.baseline.json --offset 20 --limit 20 --json
```

Run the script from your Overwatch checkout or use its installed absolute path. `--check` matches the check, command, or subproject; `--match` searches issue names, both without case sensitivity. The view groups by check and pages the initial snapshot, with links to full logs. It does not rerun checks or silently accept new regressions. When a runner does not name individual issues, the inventory contains its summary and full log instead. An increase in its reported error, warning, or failure counts is treated as a regression; a replacement issue with the same count cannot be distinguished without issue names.

# [Failure gates and protected actions]($heading.id('failure-gates-and-protected-actions'))

Failure notifications and permission decisions are separate. Listing, searching, reading, editing, and running checks are ordinary operations: a red check does not deny them or launch a check before they run. Post-tool notifications never deny a tool. Completion stays guarded until the relevant failures clear; deduplicating a notification does not clear that gate. Warnings retain each check's existing failure policy, including Zanity warnings.

There are no protected tool actions by default. Configure specific actions in the repository's `.overwatch.json`:

```json
{
  "gate": {
    "inherited": "allow",
    "protected_actions": [
      { "tool": "Bash", "command": "\\bgit\\s+push\\b", "checks": ["zanity"] },
      { "tool": "Publish", "checks": ["zanity", "go"] }
    ]
  }
}
```

Tool names match without case sensitivity; use the tool name your agent sends, such as `Bash`, `bash`, or `exec_command`. An optional `command` regular expression matches its `command` or `cmd` argument. Use syntax supported by both Python and JavaScript. Protect the specific publication or deployment command rather than the whole shell tool, so repairs remain possible. Without `command`, the rule protects the entire named tool. `checks` selects watcher names or languages; an omitted or empty list selects all checks. An unrelated failed check does not block an action scoped to passing checks. Every protected attempt checks the failure state independently, so a prior denial does not grant the next attempt.

`inherited` defaults to `"allow"`, preserving backlog triage. Set it to `"block"` to require inherited findings to clear before completion or protected actions. Ordinary operations remain available under either policy. A successful check clears its gate; it does not clear failures from other checks. Environment errors retain their existing reporting behavior.

Command hooks use `PreToolUse` only for protected-action permission decisions and `Stop` for completion. Ordinary pre-tool hooks return an empty response without rerunning checks. Claude Code's in-process plugin denies protected tools before execution and replaces guarded completion with a correction request. It requests another correction for the same findings only after a tool call completes. Repeated completion attempts remain guarded without queuing duplicate corrections. Aborted or unsuccessful turns may settle. After interruption, automatic wakeups wait until another turn starts. Protected tools remain guarded. OpenCode and Pi use their before-tool hooks for protected actions. Reload the plugin after updating hook definitions; Codex may require renewed hook trust.

# [TCR mode]($heading.id('tcr-mode'))

Set `"mode": "tcr"` in `.overwatch.json` to run test && commit || revert. After each edit of the agent that changes files, Overwatch reads the check results. If no check has a new failure, it commits every file that `.gitignore` does not ignore, on the current branch, as `tcr: <files>`. If a check has a new failure, it restores the files to the latest commit, removes the files the agent added, and tells the agent which failure caused the revert and which files came back. Failures that the session baseline already had never cause a revert. With `gate.inherited` set to `"block"`, a failing inherited check prevents the commit and the revert. A result is ignored when the files changed again while the checks ran. The `git` upstream check never causes a revert, because a new upstream commit is not an edit of the agent.

```json
{
  "mode": "tcr"
}
```

TCR starts only on a clean tree, on a branch that has a commit, with no merge, rebase, cherry-pick, revert or bisect in progress and no untracked nested repository. Otherwise Overwatch tells the agent that TCR is unavailable and changes nothing. Overwatch checks again at the start of each turn in Codex and OpenCode 2; Pi and OpenCode 1 report only the start of a session, so there TCR does not start again after it became unavailable. TCR assumes the agent is the only writer: a revert discards every change that is not committed. When Overwatch sees files change at a tool that does not edit, it makes TCR unavailable until the next turn start, so a revert cannot discard those files. It cannot tell a person's edit from the agent's when both come before the same edit tool, so do not edit the working tree, or run a second agent in it, while TCR runs. The repository's commit hooks and signing run. If a checkpoint commit fails, the changes stay staged, nothing is reverted, and TCR is unavailable until it can start again. A check that fails while no file differs from the latest commit is reported, and nothing is reverted. With `gate.inherited` set to `"block"`, an edit that leaves an inherited check failing is neither committed nor reverted, so the working tree is dirty at the next turn start and TCR becomes unavailable until you commit. TCR works in Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and Pi, and each of them commits and reverts as described and tells the agent why a revert happened. OpenCode 1 shows the "TCR is on" notice to you but not to the agent. In Claude Code, the long-running watchers report when they like, and one may stay silent for an edit, so TCR does not wait for them. A short while after an edit changed files, it runs every check once, as the Codex hook does: a check with a baseline command to its end, a watch-mode check until its first report, and judges the edit by those results. The watchers keep reporting for the guard. A new failure reverts the edit. A check that cannot run, a failing check with no earlier result of its own to compare with, and a failure with no file to restore hold the edit uncommitted and unreverted, and the agent is told why. A newer edit, or a change of the files while the checks run, makes the judgement start again or act on nothing. Claude Code runs `hooks/checkpoint.py` with `python3`, which must be on PATH, to compare files and to commit and revert; without it TCR is unavailable and says why. A check with a baseline command also reruns for the guard, so it runs twice for each edit. A revert removes empty directories, and a rename that only changes letter case is invisible to it on a case-insensitive filesystem.
